<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tepanecs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tepanec</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tepanecs were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> whose state, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a>, dominated the region in the early fifteenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> initially served them as tributaries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> warriors. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> describe how the Tepanec succession crisis after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a>'s death in 1426 led to the war in which the Mexica and their allies destroyed Tepanec hegemony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tepanecs.jpg" alt="Tepanecs" width="320" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glifo_gentilicio_tepaneca_codice_azcatitlan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tepanecs arrived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> in the late 12th or early 13th century. They were a sister people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acolhua">Acolhua</a>, speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> and sharing the same general pantheon, though some authors suspect partly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a> or Matlatzinca origins. Their patron god was Ototontecuhtli, also called Cuecuex, a major deity among the Otomi, Matlatzinca and Mazahua as well. Their name comes from their mythical city of origin, Tepanohuayan or Tepano, drawn as a stone, and their lands were called Tepanecapan.</p><p>Tradition compiled by several historians counts them among the seven tribes that set out from Chicomoztoc, the Seven Caves, whose location is uncertain. Mid-20th-century opinion placed it at La Quemada; later investigators look north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>, towards ancient Tula, or to the hill of Chiconauhtla south of Teotihuacan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to tradition the Chichimec ruler Xolotl welcomed them to the valley, and they settled on the west shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>, where under their tlatoani Acolnahuacatl they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a> from its earlier inhabitants. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a> they controlled nearly all of the valley and parts of the Toluca and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a> valleys; native sources say he lived past 100.</p><p>After his death his sons Tayatzin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxtla">Maxtla</a> came to the throne, and Maxtla most likely poisoned his brother. In 1428 Maxtla was overthrown by the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Triple Alliance</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acolhua">Acolhua</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> and his fellow Tepanecs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a>, which became the leading Tepanec city, though smaller and less prestigious than its two partners. When the Spaniard conquistadores arrived, the Tepanecs were subject to the alliance and no longer an independent people; they are known from accounts drawn from the codex traditions and compiled by novohispanic historians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tepanec” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They were among the weakest groups in the valley, latecomers who received the island because no established power wanted it, and they served as tributaries and mercenaries of the dominant <b>Tepanec</b> city of Azcapotzalco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1428, according to Texcocan tradition, its warriors joined the coalition with which Nezahualcoyotl of Texcoco and Itzcoatl of Tenochtitlan overthrew the <b>Tepanecs</b> of Azcapotzalco, but the alliance created by that victory soon became Tlaxcala’s chief enemy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Tepanecs</b> settled on the western shore of Lake Texcoco, with their capital at Azcapotzalco, in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/tepanec-empire">Tepanec Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Tlacopan (modern Tacuba) was a <b>Tepanec</b> town subject to Azcapotzalco.</blockquote><a href="/state/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a></li></ul></section>
